/// \defgroup grp_w1021_profile_result_writers Profile library result writers: CSV and Graphviz DOT
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief The profile library's three built-in result writers, which turn
///        the profiler's data into CSV tables and Graphviz call graphs when
///        the process exits.
///
/// A result writer is an object implementing ProfileResultInterface: a
/// WriteResults function that writes a file and a Delete function that
/// destroys the object. The profile library (\ref grp_w1_profile_lib)
/// registers three writer factories at startup, in GetClockCyclesFast:
///
/// | Registration name | Class | Optional arguments | Default output |
/// |---|---|---|---|
/// | `file_csv` | ProfileResultFileCSV | file name | `profile-high.csv`, plus one file per profiled thread |
/// | `file_dot` | ProfileResultFileDOT | file name, frame name, fold threshold | `profile.dot` |
/// | `file_gtt_dot` | ProfileResultFileGTT | file name, frame name, percentage threshold | `profile_gtt.dot` |
///
/// ProfileResultFileGTT is the reconstruction's own name for a writer that
/// Zero Hour does not have (see the warning on the class).
///
/// **Lifecycle.**
/// - The `profile.result <name> <arguments>` debug command looks the name up
///   in the registry and calls the factory (the writer's static `Create`)
///   with the arguments that follow the name. The factory allocates the
///   object with ProfileAllocMemory and constructs it in place; the command
///   object (ProfileCmdInterface) keeps it in its list of requested writers.
/// - At exit, ProfileShutdown calls ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions,
///   which calls each requested writer's WriteResults and then its Delete.
///   If no writer was requested, it first issues `profile.result file_csv`
///   itself (see that function for what is established about this in
///   BFME 2).
/// - Several writers, and several copies of one writer, can be requested
///   in one run. They run in request order. Each writer that writes opens
///   its file with mode `"wt"`, replacing any earlier contents, so when two
///   writers that both write use the same file name, only the later one's
///   output remains. A writer that returns before opening its file, as the
///   DOT and GTT writers do in retail, leaves an earlier writer's file
///   untouched (see ProfileResultFileDOT::WriteResults and
///   ProfileResultFileGTT::WriteResults).
///
/// **Two kinds of data.** The writers read two sources:
/// - the *function-level* profiler (ProfileFuncLevel): per thread, per
///   function, call counts, times and callers. The DOT and GTT writers and
///   the per-thread CSV files use only this;
/// - the *high-level* profiler (ProfileHighLevel): named measurements,
///   each with a unit, a total and a value per recorded frame. Only the
///   CSV writer reads it, into `profile-high.csv`.
///
/// Function-level data can be read for all frames together or for one
/// recorded frame, such as `newgame:3` (Profile::GetFrameName). In Zero
/// Hour's sources the times are CPU ticks, "global" time includes the
/// time spent in callees, and "pure" time does not.
///
/// **In retail BFME 2 the function-level data is empty.** Zero Hour's
/// header says ProfileFuncLevel exists in every build but returns empty
/// data outside profiling builds. In the retail binary, every
/// ProfileFuncLevel accessor these writers call reaches a body that
/// returns false, zero, a null pointer or an empty caller list; several are
/// shared with unrelated empty functions. As a result, in retail:
/// - `file_dot` and `file_gtt_dot` find no thread and return without
///   creating a file;
/// - `file_csv` without a file name writes no per-thread file, only
///   `profile-high.csv` with the high-level data;
/// - `file_csv` with a file name writes that file with the column header
///   and no rows, and does not write `profile-high.csv`.
///
/// The writers' own code for function-level output is in the retail binary;
/// only the data is missing.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here is data-driven: no INI block, map script or UI controls
///   the writers, and the file names, formats and limits described here are
///   fixed in code. The only control is the `profile` debug-command group
///   (ProfileCmdInterface), reached through the debug library
///   (\ref grp_w1_debug_lib), with commands of the form
///   `profile.result <writer name> <arguments>`.
/// - The writers run only at process exit and read only profiler data, so
///   they have no effect on game logic, lockstep, saves or replays.
/// - The file names are passed to the C runtime's `fopen` unchanged, so a
///   relative name, including every default, is relative to the process's
///   current directory, not to the game's data folders.
/// - Of the three, only `file_csv` produces data from the retail build, and
///   only when given no file name.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The function-level profiler is the missing piece: a port that wants
///   per-function CSV files and call graphs must supply real ProfileFuncLevel
///   data (Zero Hour compiles its full implementation only in its Profile
///   build configuration), or feed these formats from a modern profiler.
/// - The output code is plain C stdio. It prints 64-bit counts with the
///   Microsoft-specific `%%I64i` format, and function addresses are 32-bit
///   values printed as 8 hex digits, so a non-Microsoft C runtime or a
///   64-bit build needs those formats and types changed.
/// - None of the writers frees its copied file and frame names, and the GTT
///   writer does not free its visited-edge list: Delete releases only the
///   object. This is harmless because the writers run at exit.
///
/// \see \ref grp_w1_profile_lib, \ref sub_common_services


/// \file Libraries/Source/profile/internal_result.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_profile_result_writers
/// \brief Declares the three built-in result writers: ProfileResultFileCSV,
///        ProfileResultFileDOT and ProfileResultFileGTT.
///
/// Zero Hour's header of the same name declares the CSV and DOT writers;
/// BFME 2's version adds the CSV writer's optional file name and the GTT
/// writer. The common base,
/// ProfileResultInterface, is declared in `profile_result.h`.


/// \file Libraries/Source/profile/profile_result.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_profile_result_writers
/// \brief The bodies of the three built-in result writers.
///
/// Zero Hour's `profile_result.cpp` rebuilt for BFME 2, with BFME 2's
/// changes to the CSV and DOT writers and the GTT writer that Zero Hour
/// lacks. It also holds a file-local helper, whose original name is not
/// known, that makes a function name safe for a CSV field (used by
/// ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteThread), and the GTT writer's recursive graph
/// walk, a private member function of ProfileResultFileGTT whose original
/// name is also not known (called by ProfileResultFileGTT::WriteResults).


/// \class ProfileResultFileCSV
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_profile_result_writers
/// \brief Result writer `file_csv`: writes the profiler's data as
///        comma-separated tables, one file per profiled thread plus a
///        summary of the high-level measurements.
///
/// The default writer: when nothing else was requested,
/// ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions issues the command that requests
/// it. It is created by ProfileResultFileCSV::Create from the
/// `profile.result file_csv` command, lives in memory from
/// ProfileAllocMemory, and is destroyed by ProfileResultFileCSV::Delete
/// right after ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteResults has run at exit. Its only
/// state is an optional output file name, copied at construction.
///
/// Output, depending on that name:
/// - **No name:** one file per profiled thread, named `prof<thread id>-all.csv`
///   with the id as 8 hex digits, then `profile-high.csv` with the
///   high-level measurements (format under ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteResults).
/// - **A name:** only the thread with the most profiled functions, into
///   that file. No `profile-high.csv` is written.
///
/// \par Per-thread file format
/// A private member function writes each thread's file.
/// - Header: `Function,File`, then `Call count,PTT (all),GTT (all),PT/C (all),GT/C (all),Caller (all)`,
///   then `Call (f),PTT (f),GTT (f),PT/C (f),GT/C (f),Caller (f)` with
///   `f` replaced by each frame's name, for every frame
///   Profile::GetFrameCount reports.
/// - One row per profiled function. It starts with `name[address]` (the
///   address as 8 hex digits) and `source#line`.
/// - Then, for all frames together and then for each frame: the call
///   count, pure total time, global total time, pure time per call, global
///   time per call, and the callers. A function with no calls in that frame
///   gets six empty fields.
/// - The callers field is a space-separated list of `name[address](count)`.
///   When a function has more than 50 callers, the list is cut down: a
///   count threshold, starting at 1, is raised to the smallest remaining
///   count until no more than 50 callers are above it. Those callers are
///   listed and the others are summed into a final `misc(count)` entry. A
///   capped list can therefore hold fewer than 50 entries, and never lists
///   a caller that made only one call.
/// - Commas in function names are replaced with semicolons, and names
///   longer than 299 characters are truncated. Source file names are
///   written unchanged.
///
/// \warning A per-thread file is opened without a check: if it cannot be
///          created, the writes go through a null file pointer, which the
///          C runtime does not allow, as in Zero Hour. Giving the writer a
///          file name the process cannot create is therefore unsafe.
///
/// \note Differences from Zero Hour, from both bodies, include the
///       following. Zero Hour's writer takes no arguments, always writes
///       every thread and the summary, and separates fields with tabs. It
///       writes the source and line as `source, line`, which a
///       comma-separated file would split in two, and it prints function
///       and caller names unchanged. BFME 2 adds the optional file name,
///       uses commas, replaces commas in function and caller names with
///       semicolons and truncates those names to 299 characters, caps each
///       caller list, and skips the summary when `profile-high.csv` cannot
///       be created, where Zero Hour would write through a null file.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileDOT, ProfileResultFileGTT,
///      ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions


/// \fn ProfileResultFileCSV::ProfileResultFileCSV(const char *fileName)
/// \brief Creates a CSV writer, keeping a private copy of the optional
///        output file name.
///
/// Called only by ProfileResultFileCSV::Create. Zero Hour's constructor
/// takes no arguments; the file name is BFME 2's addition.
///
/// \param fileName Output file for the busiest thread, or null for the
///                 default per-thread files and `profile-high.csv`. The
///                 string is copied into memory from ProfileAllocMemory,
///                 which is never freed.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteResults


/// \fn ProfileResultInterface *ProfileResultFileCSV::Create(int argn, const char *const *argv)
/// \brief Factory registered as `file_csv`: creates a CSV writer from the
///        `profile.result file_csv` command's arguments.
///
/// GetClockCyclesFast registers it with
/// ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction, and the `result` command calls it
/// with the arguments that follow the writer name.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Allocates the object with ProfileAllocMemory and constructs it there
/// with placement `new`, passing the first argument as the file name, or
/// null when there is none. Further arguments are ignored. The registered
/// help string for `file_csv` is empty, so the `result` command's listing
/// does not mention the optional file name.
///
/// \param argn Number of arguments after the writer name.
/// \param argv Those arguments.
/// \return The new writer.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's factory ignores its arguments.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileCSV::ProfileResultFileCSV, ProfileResultFileDOT::Create,
///      ProfileResultFileGTT::Create


/// \fn void ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteThread(ProfileFuncLevel::Thread &thread)
/// \brief Writes one thread's function-level data as a CSV table: one row
///        per profiled function, one group of six columns for all frames
///        and for each recorded frame.
///
/// A private member, called by ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteResults once per
/// thread, or once for the busiest thread when the writer has a file name.
/// The file format is described on the ProfileResultFileCSV class.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The file is the writer's file name if it has one, else
///   `prof<thread id>-all.csv`, with the id from
///   ProfileFuncLevel::Thread::GetId.
/// - Function names, of each profiled function and of each listed caller,
///   pass through a small helper that copies them into one fixed static
///   buffer, truncating them, and replaces every comma with a semicolon,
///   so names such as template instances do not split a field.
/// - The frame loop starts at the all-frames value (ProfileFuncLevel::Id
///   has a `Total` value for it, the largest unsigned number) and wraps to
///   frame 0, which is why the totals come first.
///
/// \param thread The thread to write.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteResults, Profile::GetFrameName


/// \fn void ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteResults(void)
/// \brief Writes the CSV results at exit: either every thread plus the
///        high-level summary, or only the busiest thread into the named
///        file.
///
/// Called through the writer's virtual table by
/// ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions, which then calls
/// ProfileResultFileCSV::Delete.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - **With a file name:** counts the profiled functions of every thread
///   and writes the thread with the most (the first one on a tie, thread 0
///   when none has any) with ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteThread, then
///   returns. BFME 2 only; Zero Hour has no file name.
/// - **Without one:** writes every thread with
///   ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteThread, then creates `profile-high.csv`.
///   If that fails, it returns; otherwise it writes the header
///   `Profile,Unit,total` followed by every frame name, and one row per
///   high-level measurement (ProfileHighLevel): its name, its unit, its
///   total and its value in each frame, with an empty field where it has
///   no value for that frame. The values are the strings the
///   high-level profiler formats; they are not escaped.
///
/// \note In retail BFME 2 the function-level profiler returns no threads,
///       so without a file name only `profile-high.csv` is written, and with
///       one the file gets only the column header (see
///       \ref grp_w1021_profile_result_writers).
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteThread, ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions


/// \fn const char *ProfileResultFileCSV::GetName(void) const
/// \brief Returns `file_csv`, the writer's registration name.
///
/// Zero Hour declares the same inline virtual function. Neither
/// ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions nor the `result` command calls
/// it: the command identifies writers by the name in its registry.
///
/// \return The string `file_csv`.
///
/// \see ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction


/// \fn void ProfileResultFileCSV::Delete(void)
/// \brief Destroys the writer and frees its memory with ProfileFreeMemory.
///
/// ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions calls it right after
/// ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteResults. The class has no destructor of its
/// own, so the copied file name is not freed.
///
/// \note The three writers' Delete functions compile to identical code, and
///       retail BFME 2 keeps a single shared body for all of them.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileDOT::Delete, ProfileResultFileGTT::Delete


/// \class ProfileResultFileDOT
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_profile_result_writers
/// \brief Result writer `file_dot`: writes one thread's call graph as a
///        Graphviz DOT file, with a node per called function and an edge
///        per caller, or a node per source file when the graph is folded.
///
/// Created by ProfileResultFileDOT::Create from the
/// `profile.result file_dot` command and run at exit like the other
/// writers (\ref grp_w1021_profile_result_writers). It keeps three settings
/// from construction: the output file name, an optional frame name that
/// limits the graph to one recorded frame, and a fold threshold. When more
/// functions than the threshold were called, the writer switches to a
/// folded graph that groups functions by source file, with at most 200
/// functions kept per file.
///
/// Zero Hour's header says the result is meant for the `dot` tool from the
/// Graphviz package, for example `dot -Tgif -ograph.gif profile.dot`.
///
/// \note Differences from Zero Hour, from both bodies, include: the default
///       fold threshold is 999 instead of 0; an unfolded graph draws open
///       arrowheads instead of none; unfolded edge labels pad the call count
///       with four trailing spaces; the fold comparison is signed, so a
///       negative threshold always folds in BFME 2 and never folds in Zero
///       Hour; and a folded graph lists the source files without the
///       file-to-file edges Zero Hour writes (see
///       ProfileResultFileDOT::WriteResults).
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileGTT, ProfileResultFileCSV


/// \fn ProfileResultFileDOT::ProfileResultFileDOT(const char *fileName, const char *frameName, int foldThreshold)
/// \brief Creates a DOT writer, copying its file and frame names.
///
/// Called only by ProfileResultFileDOT::Create. The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param fileName      Output file; null selects `profile.dot`. Copied
///                      into memory from ProfileAllocMemory.
/// \param frameName     Name of the recorded frame to graph, such as
///                      `newgame:3`, or null for all frames together.
///                      Copied likewise.
/// \param foldThreshold Number of called functions above which the graph
///                      is folded by source file.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileDOT::WriteResults


/// \fn ProfileResultInterface *ProfileResultFileDOT::Create(int argn, const char *const *argv)
/// \brief Factory registered as `file_dot`: creates a DOT writer from the
///        `profile.result file_dot` command's arguments.
///
/// The arguments are positional, as the registered help string
/// `[ file [ frame_name [ fold_threshold ] ] ]` shows: a missing file or
/// frame name becomes null, and the fold threshold is read with `atoi` and
/// defaults to 999. Further arguments are ignored. The object is allocated
/// with ProfileAllocMemory and constructed in place.
///
/// \param argn Number of arguments after the writer name.
/// \param argv Those arguments.
/// \return The new writer.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's default fold threshold is 0, which folds any graph
///       with a called function. Zero Hour also registers the CSV writer's
///       factory under `file_dot` (see GetClockCyclesFast); BFME 2
///       registers this one.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileDOT::ProfileResultFileDOT, ProfileResultFileGTT::Create


/// \fn void ProfileResultFileDOT::WriteResults(void)
/// \brief Writes the call graph of the busiest thread, for one frame or
///        all frames, as a DOT file.
///
/// Called through the writer's virtual table by
/// ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions at exit.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - If there is no profiled thread, it returns without creating a file.
///   This is what happens in retail BFME 2 (see
///   \ref grp_w1021_profile_result_writers).
/// - It picks a "main" thread with a selection loop taken unchanged from
///   Zero Hour. Zero Hour's header says it picks the thread with the most
///   functions, but the loop does not reliably do that; for example it
///   never considers the second thread.
/// - The frame name is compared exactly with each recorded frame's name;
///   with no match, or no frame name, the totals over all frames are used.
/// - It counts the functions called in that frame, opens the file (and
///   returns if it cannot) and writes a left-to-right `digraph` header with
///   box-shaped nodes in Arial and blue edge labels.
/// - **Unfolded** (no more called functions than the fold threshold): one
///   node per called function, named `f` plus its address in hex and
///   labelled with the function name, then one edge from each caller to
///   the function, labelled with the call count followed by four spaces
///   (Zero Hour's label has no padding). Arrowheads are open.
/// - The fold test compares the count of called functions with the
///   threshold as signed integers, so a negative threshold always folds.
///   Zero Hour counts in an unsigned variable, which makes the same test
///   unsigned, so there a negative threshold never folds.
/// - **Folded:** functions are grouped by source file, keeping at most 200
///   per file, and each file is written as one quoted node. The callers'
///   source files are still looked up, but no edge is written, so the
///   folded graph has nodes only. Arrowheads are declared closed. Zero
///   Hour writes no node lines here; it writes edges between source files
///   instead.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileGTT::WriteResults, ProfileResultFileCSV::WriteResults


/// \fn const char *ProfileResultFileDOT::GetName(void) const
/// \brief Returns `file_dot`, the writer's registration name.
///
/// Zero Hour declares the same inline virtual function. Neither
/// ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions nor the `result` command calls
/// it.
///
/// \return The string `file_dot`.
///
/// \see ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction


/// \fn void ProfileResultFileDOT::Delete(void)
/// \brief Destroys the writer and frees its memory with ProfileFreeMemory.
///
/// ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions calls it right after
/// ProfileResultFileDOT::WriteResults. The class has no destructor of its
/// own, so the copied file and frame names are not freed. Retail BFME 2
/// uses this one body for all three writers' Delete functions, whose code
/// is identical.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileCSV::Delete, ProfileResultFileGTT::Delete


/// \class ProfileResultFileGTT
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_profile_result_writers
/// \brief Result writer `file_gtt_dot`: writes a Graphviz DOT call tree that
///        follows the most expensive calls down from the function with the
///        largest total time.
///
/// \warning Name not confirmed: ProfileResultFileGTT and its member names
///          are the reconstruction's own. The CSV and DOT writers take their
///          names from Zero Hour's source, but Zero Hour has no such writer
///          and the retail binary carries no symbol for it. Only its
///          registration name `file_gtt_dot`, its strings and its behaviour
///          come from retail. BFME 1 cannot show whether it had one
///          (\ref grp_w1_profile_lib). The names may change.
///
/// BFME 2's addition to the result writers. Where the DOT writer draws
/// every call, this one draws only the calls that account for a meaningful
/// share of the time: an edge from a caller to a callee is written only when
/// the callee's global time apportioned to that caller (by call count), as a
/// share of the root function's global time, reaches a threshold. That share
/// is computed approximately, as described under
/// ProfileResultFileGTT::WriteResults, and can overstate the true share when
/// the root's time is small, so an edge whose true share is below the
/// threshold can still be drawn. The `gtt` in its name presumably refers to
/// that global total time, which the CSV writer's columns call GTT.
///
/// Created by ProfileResultFileGTT::Create from the
/// `profile.result file_gtt_dot` command and run at exit like the other
/// writers. Its state is the output file name, an optional frame name, the
/// percentage threshold, and a growable list of the edges already written
/// (ProfileResultFileGTT::MarkVisited), which stops the walk from repeating
/// an edge or looping on recursive calls.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileDOT, ProfileResultFileCSV


/// \fn ProfileResultFileGTT::ProfileResultFileGTT(const char *fileName, const char *frameName, int percentThreshold)
/// \brief Creates a GTT writer: copies its file and frame names and starts
///        with an empty visited-edge list.
///
/// \warning Name not confirmed; see ProfileResultFileGTT.
///
/// Called only by ProfileResultFileGTT::Create. The body has the same shape
/// as ProfileResultFileDOT::ProfileResultFileDOT, plus the empty edge list.
///
/// \param fileName         Output file; null selects `profile_gtt.dot`.
///                         Copied into memory from ProfileAllocMemory.
/// \param frameName        Name of the recorded frame to graph, or null for
///                         all frames together. Copied likewise.
/// \param percentThreshold Threshold for the computed edge share, in
///                         hundredths of a percent of the root's time (100
///                         is 1%). The computed share can overstate the true
///                         share when the root's time is small (see
///                         ProfileResultFileGTT::WriteResults).
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileGTT::WriteResults


/// \fn ProfileResultInterface *ProfileResultFileGTT::Create(int argn, const char *const *argv)
/// \brief Factory registered as `file_gtt_dot`: creates a GTT writer from
///        the `profile.result file_gtt_dot` command's arguments.
///
/// \warning Name not confirmed; see ProfileResultFileGTT.
///
/// The arguments are positional, as the registered help string
/// `[ file [ frame_name [ percent_threshold, 10000=100%, def=1% ] ] ]`
/// shows: a missing file or frame name becomes null, and the threshold is
/// read with `atoi` and defaults to 100, that is 1%. Further arguments are
/// ignored. The object is allocated with ProfileAllocMemory and
/// constructed in place.
///
/// \param argn Number of arguments after the writer name.
/// \param argv Those arguments.
/// \return The new writer.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileGTT::ProfileResultFileGTT, ProfileResultFileDOT::Create


/// \fn void ProfileResultFileGTT::WriteResults(void)
/// \brief Writes the GTT call tree of the busiest thread, for one frame or
///        all frames, as a DOT file.
///
/// \warning Name not confirmed; see ProfileResultFileGTT.
///
/// Called through the writer's virtual table by
/// ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions at exit.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Thread and frame selection are the DOT writer's
///   (ProfileResultFileDOT::WriteResults): no thread means no file, which
///   is what happens in retail BFME 2.
/// - If no function in the thread has a recorded caller in the frame, it
///   returns without creating a file. Otherwise it opens the file (and
///   returns if it cannot) and writes a `digraph` header with box-shaped
///   nodes in Arial, open arrowheads and red edge labels.
/// - The root is the function with the largest global time in the frame
///   (the first one on a tie). A private recursive helper, whose original
///   name is not known, then walks down from it:
///   - It does nothing when the root's global time is below 10000 time
///     units (CPU ticks in Zero Hour's function-level profiler), which also
///     keeps the share below from dividing by zero.
///   - For each function that lists the current function among its
///     callers, the edge time is that function's global time multiplied by
///     the calls from this caller and divided by all its calls. The share
///     is the edge time divided by one ten-thousandth of the root's global
///     time (both integer divisions), so it is roughly the edge's share in
///     hundredths of a percent. Because the divisor is truncated first, it
///     can overstate the share when the root's time is small: for example,
///     with a root time of 15000 units the divisor is 1, so an edge equal
///     to the root's time reports 150.00%.
///   - An edge whose computed share is below the threshold, or that was
///     already written (ProfileResultFileGTT::MarkVisited), is skipped.
///     Otherwise it writes both functions as nodes labelled with the name,
///     the global time divided by one million, rounded (printed with an `M`
///     suffix), and the call count, then the edge, labelled with the edge
///     time in the same form and the computed share as a percentage with two
///     decimals, and continues from the callee.
/// - Nodes are written again for every edge that touches them; Graphviz
///   merges repeated node statements.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// ProfileResultFileGTT::MarkVisited searches the edge list linearly, so
/// the work grows with the square of the number of edges written, and the
/// walk recurses once per level of the call chains it follows. Both stay
/// small when the threshold prunes most edges; a threshold of 0 draws every
/// edge reachable from the root.
///
/// \see ProfileResultFileDOT::WriteResults, ProfileResultFileGTT::MarkVisited


/// \fn bool ProfileResultFileGTT::MarkVisited(unsigned from, unsigned to)
/// \brief Records a call-graph edge as written, and reports whether it
///        already was.
///
/// \warning Name not confirmed; see ProfileResultFileGTT.
///
/// Its only caller is the GTT writer's private recursive graph walk, a
/// helper whose original name is not known and which
/// ProfileResultFileGTT::WriteResults starts once from the root function.
/// The walk calls it for each edge that passes the percentage threshold,
/// before writing that edge. Because a recorded edge is never written or
/// followed again, the walk ends even when functions call each other
/// recursively.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The edges are kept as caller and callee pairs in an array that is
/// searched linearly. A new edge is appended; when the array is full it
/// grows by 1000 entries with ProfileReAllocMemory. The array is never
/// freed (ProfileResultFileGTT::Delete releases only the object).
///
